WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.230
Imagine a supervisor who trusts the team to sort out

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:07.510
case assignments among themselves. It sounds respectful. A few months

3
00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.230
later, the supervisor hears that newer staff and colleagues who

4
00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.290
work remotely are consistently getting the hardest cases, and nobody

5
00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.430
quite knows how that happened.

6
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.920
That is such a familiar story, and nobody in it

7
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.080
set out to be unfair. So what is the name

8
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.240
for this kind of leadership, and how does the page

9
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:22.920
describe it?

10
00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.450
It is called laissez-faire leadership, which roughly means letting things

11
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.390
happen. The leader gives little direction, little feedback, and leaves

12
00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:34.650
decisions to drift. The page is careful to separate it

13
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.110
from empowering leadership. When a leader truly delegates, they set

14
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.850
clear goals, provide resources and stay available. Laissez-faire is stepping

15
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.550
back from the leadership role itself.

16
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.860
And yet, from the outside, the two can look alike.

17
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.160
In both cases the leader is not in the middle

18
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.660
of every decision. How would a team tell the difference?

19
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:58.210
By what is missing. With real delegation, people know the

20
00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.410
goal, the limits and where to go for help. With

21
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:06.730
laissez-faire leadership, priorities are unclear, strong work goes unrecognized, problems

22
00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:11.070
go unaddressed, and requests sit unanswered. When colleagues clash, they

23
00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.670
are told to work it out among themselves.

24
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.650
Then, when a complaint finally reaches the top, the leader

25
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.610
suddenly steps in, and the team feels whiplash.

26
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.900
You said something earlier that stayed with me: nobody knew

27
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.520
how the hardest cases ended up where they did. Where

28
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.040
does the decision making actually go when the leader steps

29
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:28.460
away?

30
00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.910
Power does not disappear. It moves to whoever has the

31
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.730
most informal influence: the loudest voices, the longest tenure, the

32
00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.370
strongest networks. Those people quietly set the norms and divide

33
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.230
the work, and no one holds them accountable for it.

34
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.610
That is how the hardest cases end up with the

35
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.350
people who have the least insider knowledge.

36
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.140
I don't think most leaders choose this on purpose. It

37
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.820
often looks like trust, or like being too busy. Why

38
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.040
do people end up leading this way?

39
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.410
The page treats it as a signal rather than a

40
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.350
character flaw. A supervisor drifting into laissez-faire may be overwhelmed,

41
00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.810
may never have been trained to manage, may find conflict

42
00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.290
deeply uncomfortable, or may simply be worn out. Each of

43
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.010
those has a different remedy. Naming the reason is the

44
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:12.970
first helpful step.

45
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.280
Is there ever a setting where stepping all the way

46
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.280
back is the right call? I imagine some highly skilled

47
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.040
experts want very little interference.

48
00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.730
In a few narrow cases, yes. Senior experts with deep

49
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.170
mastery, clear outside standards and no need to coordinate with

50
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.730
others can benefit from being left alone to do original

51
00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:35.730
work. But across almost every team in public service, research

52
00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.550
links hands-off leadership to the lowest job satisfaction, productivity and

53
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.670
clarity about roles.

54
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.360
Let's talk about who pays the price. The summary on

55
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.760
the page says the staff with the least insider knowledge

56
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.900
pay the most. What does that mean in practice?

57
00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.450
It means unwritten rules start to decide everything. Newer staff,

58
00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.090
people who are the first in their family to work

59
00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.490
in a large organization, and neurodivergent colleagues who do best

60
00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.470
with clear, explicit expectations are left guessing, and then judged

61
00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.230
at review time against standards nobody ever explained.

62
00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:12.650
Colleagues who share the background of the informal leaders tend

63
00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.850
to thrive, while others are quietly left out of projects

64
00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:16.710
and mentoring.

65
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.360
What about the harder situations, when someone experiences disrespect from

66
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.080
a colleague, or needs an accommodation to do their job?

67
00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:28.110
That is where the harm is sharpest. When disrespect goes

68
00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.410
unaddressed and the person is told to handle it directly,

69
00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:32.950
the whole burden falls on the person who was harmed.

70
00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.630
Accommodation requests also need a supervisor who actively pushes them

71
00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.050
through, and without one they can sit in a queue

72
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:39.470
for months.

73
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:44.070
And equity goals for the unit simply gather dust, because

74
00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.890
nobody is setting expectations or tracking progress.

75
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.920
So let's return to the supervisor with the uneven case

76
00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.540
assignments. What would stepping back in look like, without swinging

77
00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.960
all the way into micromanaging?

78
00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.950
Setting a clear, open approach to assignments with the team,

79
00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.530
and checking it regularly for fairness. That is not taking

80
00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.490
control of everyone's work. It is taking responsibility for the

81
00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:11.130
rules everyone works within. Clear criteria protect everyone, especially the

82
00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.230
people with the least influence.

83
00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.900
That is encouraging, because the fix is not to become

84
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.960
controlling. It is to become present. What else does the

85
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:22.200
page suggest for leaders and for organizations?

86
00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.370
For leaders: regular one-on-one conversations, written expectations for each role,

87
00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:32.710
timely feedback, and active follow-through on accommodation requests. Remember that

88
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.670
stepping back from how the work is done never means

89
00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.770
stepping back from goals, standards, barrier removal or team culture.

90
00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:43.370
For organizations: watch for unexplained turnover, slow accommodations or falling

91
00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.070
engagement by unit, so drift is noticed early and supervisors

92
00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:47.890
get support.

93
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.500
And for someone listening who realizes they have drifted into

94
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.660
this, the message seems hopeful. You do not have to

95
00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.880
change your personality. You have to show up a little

96
00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.460
more on purpose.

97
00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.470
That is it exactly. Here is a question to carry

98
00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.850
with you. Where has a hands-off approach left a gap

99
00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.770
that someone else is quietly filling? Thank you for listening.
